 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Lower threshold",
   "expr": "VT1 = VS × R2 / ((R1 × R3) / (R1 + R3) + R2)",
   "where": {
    "VT1": "threshold while the output is high (VIN below VT)",
    "VS": "supply voltage",
    "R1": "resistor from VS to the non-inverting input",
    "R2": "resistor from the non-inverting input to ground",
    "R3": "feedback resistor from the output to the non-inverting input"
   },
   "note": "With the output high, R3 sits in parallel with R1, and that pair is in series with R2.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Upper threshold",
   "expr": "VT2 = VS × ((R2 × R3) / (R2 + R3)) / (R1 + (R2 × R3) / (R2 + R3))",
   "where": {
    "VT2": "threshold while the output is low (VIN above VT)",
    "VS": "supply voltage",
    "R1": "resistor from VS to the non-inverting input",
    "R2": "resistor from the non-inverting input to ground",
    "R3": "feedback resistor from the output to the non-inverting input"
   },
   "note": "With the output low, R3 sits in parallel with R2, and that pair is in series with R1.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Hysteresis",
   "expr": "ΔVIN = VT1 – VT2",
   "where": {
    "ΔVIN": "hysteresis, the gap between the two switching thresholds"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "Allowed power dissipation",
   "expr": "PD = (TJ(MAX) – TA) / RθJA",
   "where": {
    "PD": "largest allowed dissipation",
    "TJ(MAX)": "maximum junction temperature, 150 °C",
    "TA": "ambient temperature",
    "RθJA": "junction-to-ambient thermal resistance, 186 °C/W"
   },
   "note": "For the part soldered directly to a PCB.",
   "page": 4
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The output is low (VOL) when the non-inverting input is below the inverting input, and high (VOH, set by the pull-up) when it is above it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input stage",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The inputs are bipolar. The common-mode range runs from 0 V up to VS – 1.5 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output stage",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The output is a single NPN transistor with its emitter at ground and its collector open, so an external pull-up to the positive supply is required. The pull-up must be large enough to keep dissipation down and small enough for the output to switch with its load connected. Open-collector outputs of several comparators can be tied together for a wired-OR, and the output can serve as a single-pole switch to ground. The output sinks roughly 10 mA; past that limit the output voltage climbs steeply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Hysteresis",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "When the two inputs are within about the offset voltage of each other the output may oscillate or chatter. Positive feedback through a three-resistor network adds hysteresis: two thresholds, one for a rising input and one for a falling input, so the input is carried quickly past the crossover. In the inverting connection the output is high while VIN is below VT."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input voltage range",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Both inputs must stay within the common-mode range, 0 V to VS – 1.5 V, for a correct comparison. Outside it: with IN- above the range and IN+ inside it, the output is low and sinking; with IN+ above the range and IN- inside it, the output is off (high impedance); with both above the range the output is low and sinking. Inside the range the output is low when IN- exceeds IN+ by more than the offset voltage and off when IN- is below IN+ by more than the offset."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Overdrive",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Overdrive is the input difference beyond the offset voltage. It must exceed the offset for an accurate decision, and larger overdrive gives a shorter response time."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output current",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The pull-up resistor and its supply set the output current, and VOL rises with that current (see the output saturation voltage curves). The output current also affects the switching transient."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypass",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "A 0.1 µF bypass capacitor close to the supply pins keeps noise from the supply out of the comparator."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Comparators pick up input noise readily. Use an unbroken, low-inductance ground plane; put a 0.1 µF ceramic surface-mount capacitor right at VS; keep input and output traces short and apart from each other; solder the part to the board rather than using a socket. With slowly moving inputs, a capacitor of 1000 pF or less across the inputs helps stop oscillation in the transition region, at some cost in propagation delay when the source impedance is low. Run top-side ground between the inputs and the output, and route the ground trace from the GND pin under the part to the bypass capacitor so it shields the inputs from the output. Keep input traces well away from supply lines; a second bypass capacitor on the negative rail is used only with split supplies."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "ESD protection is limited: store and handle the part with its leads shorted or in conductive foam."
  }
 ],
